The children prop, directly extending Module 6's brief mention into a deliberate pattern:

interface CardProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

function Card({ children }: CardProps) {
  return <div className="rounded-lg shadow p-6">{children}</div>;
}

// Used flexibly - Card has no idea what's inside, and doesn't need to
<Card><h2>Title</h2><p>Any content at all</p></Card>

Compound components — a parent component and a set of specifically-related child components that work together, sharing implicit state via Context (lesson 3 covers this mechanism fully):

<Tabs>
  <Tabs.List>
    <Tabs.Tab id="profile">Profile</Tabs.Tab>
    <Tabs.Tab id="settings">Settings</Tabs.Tab>
  </Tabs.List>
  <Tabs.Panel id="profile">Profile content</Tabs.Panel>
  <Tabs.Panel id="settings">Settings content</Tabs.Panel>
</Tabs>

Children as a function — passing a function as children, letting the parent component share data with whatever the caller chooses to render:

<DataProvider>
  {(data) => <p>{data.name}</p>}
</DataProvider>

