codingChallenge.solutionExample:
import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';

const ProductCard = React.memo(function ProductCard({
  product,
  onAddToCart
}: { product: Product; onAddToCart: (id: string) => void }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{product.name}</h3>
      <button onClick={() => onAddToCart(product.id)}>Add to Cart</button>
    </div>
  );
});

function ProductList({ products }: { products: Product[] }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleAddToCart = useCallback((id: string) => {
    console.log('Added:', id);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Unrelated counter: {count}</button>
      {products.map((product) => (
        <ProductCard key={product.id} product={product} onAddToCart={handleAddToCart} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

// BEFORE: the Profiler would show every ProductCard re-rendering every
// single time the counter button is clicked, even though no ProductCard's
// own props (product, onAddToCart) actually changed in any meaningful way.
// AFTER: the Profiler would show ZERO ProductCard re-renders when the
// counter changes, since React.memo's shallow comparison now correctly
// detects that product and the now-stable handleAddToCart reference are
// both unchanged between renders.

sections.content:
Without code splitting — every component's code is included in the initial bundle, regardless of whether it's needed immediately:

import AdminDashboard from './AdminDashboard'; // loaded for EVERY user,
  // even ones who will never visit the admin dashboard at all

With code splitting, using React.lazy and Suspense:

import { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';

const AdminDashboard = lazy(() => import('./AdminDashboard'));
// AdminDashboard's code is now in its own separate bundle, downloaded
// ONLY when this component actually needs to render

function App() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading...</p>}>
      <AdminDashboard />
    </Suspense>
  );
}

Suspense's fallback prop directly extends Module 6, lesson 5's loading-state pattern — it's shown automatically while the lazily-loaded component's separate bundle is still being downloaded, with no manual loading state management required.

