// REVIEW FINDINGS:

// CHECK 1 - Composition: TeamMemberCard has grown to accept 9 different
// boolean props (showEmail, showRole, showAvatar, compactMode, ...) to
// handle different display contexts across the app.
// FINDING: This is exactly the signal lesson 1 warned about - refactored
// to accept children/composition instead, letting each usage compose
// its own specific display needs rather than configuring through props.

// CHECK 2 - Logic extraction: useState+useEffect for fetching team data
// appears, nearly identically, in TeamPage, TeamSettingsPage, and
// TeamReportsPage.
// FINDING: Genuine duplication confirmed - extracted into a single
// useTeamData(teamId) custom hook, used by all three pages.

// CHECK 3 - Context scope: Profiler recording shows that toggling a
// notification badge re-renders the entire team member list, despite
// the list having nothing to do with notifications.
// FINDING: Both pieces of state were bundled into one TeamContext.
// Split into a separate NotificationsContext and TeamContext.

// CHECK 4 - Memoization audit: TeamMemberCard was wrapped in React.memo
// six months ago, but Profiler shows it still re-renders on every parent
// render anyway, since its onClick prop is recreated fresh each time.
// FINDING: React.memo alone was insufficient - added the missing
// useCallback pairing on the parent's handler, exactly per lesson 4.

// CHECK 5 - Bundle size: Bundle analysis shows TeamReportsPage's charting
// library accounts for 40% of the application's total bundle size,
// despite only 5% of users ever visiting that specific page.
// FINDING: Strong, evidence-based code-splitting candidate - converted
// to React.lazy + Suspense, exactly per lesson 5.

// VERDICT: 5 genuine architectural issues found and fixed through this
// systematic review, none of which would have been caught by simply
// confirming the feature 'works' in casual testing.
